{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject only when the test's installation ritual makes the subject's failure visible before the test can pass, and describes its subject when the subject's own guard structure already determines the pass/fail distribution.","counterconditions":["If the shown-to-fail-first order is not actually enforced by compile.py but is only a report ordering convention, the asymmetry collapses.","If the 1/9 mutation was a content-edit hole not reachable by the test harness's mutation operators, the loss is an artifact of mutation selection, not a property of the test.","If the ordinarily-guarded modules had guards written after the tests, the description/constraint attribution is reversed."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01cc9b7f77380bf9","quote":"| GAP REG-1 | 0/6 |"},{"block":"002779d3ed9dd0a6"}],"mechanism":"In the shown-to-fail-first installation, the mutation is planted so that the test must fail first. This forces the test to constrain the mutated subject because the failure is a prerequisite for the test being counted. In the ordinarily-guarded modules, the existing guards (e.g. registry.py's 6/6) already encode the failure modes, so the test merely describes what the guard has already constrained.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.72},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject only if the test's failure conditions are independent of the subject's own validation logic; otherwise the test describes a tautology, and the 1/9 loss occurs because compile.py's shown-to-fail-first installation accidentally aligns the test's failure with the subject's self-validation.","counterconditions":["If compile.py's mutation operators cannot distinguish self-validation from independent assertion, the 1/9 loss is an instrumentation limit, not a description/constraint distinction.","If the ordinarily-guarded modules' 4/4 to 6/7 rates arise from guard diversity rather than test independence, the tautology claim fails.","If the lost mutation was in a code path not executed by any test, the loss is a coverage gap, not a description relation."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"028950f9751ab59a","quote":"The error was not noise, it was **masking**: mutations no guard catches were reported as caught."},{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12"}],"mechanism":"When a test imports the same validation checker the subject uses, the test's verdict is derived from the subject's own verdict function. Under shown-to-fail-first installation, the planted mutation fails the subject's self-check, so the test reports the failure, but this is description (restating the subject's self-report) rather than constraint. The 3-of-26 result distribution shows that only mutations escaping the subject's self-check are true constraints; the 1/9 lost mutation was one such escape that compile.py's installation order masked.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject only when the test is installed before the subject's guard registry is populated; after the registry is populated, the test describes the registry's verdicts, and the 1-of-9 loss occurs because compile.py's shown-to-fail-first order bypasses the empty-registry state.","counterconditions":["If all guards in the ordinarily-guarded modules are lazy, the import-time claim fails.","If the 1/9 mutation was lost because the test file itself was not run, the registry order is irrelevant.","If compile.py's installation order is not deterministic, the observed distribution is not a stable property."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01cc9b7f77380bf9","quote":"| 6/6 | `src/poietics/pff/registry.py` |"},{"block":"0a5a41977d58bdb4"}],"mechanism":"The installation ritual determines whether the test sees an unguarded subject (constraint) or an already-guarded subject (description). Shown-to-fail-first installation means the mutation is planted before the test runs, but if the subject's guard registry is populated at import time, the test never sees the unguarded failure mode. The distribution 1/9 lost vs 4/4 to 6/7 guarded reflects how many subjects have import-time guard registration versus lazy guard registration.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.58},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject only if the test's assertions are independent of the subject's declared data; when a guard checks declared data, the test describes that declaration, and the 1/9 loss is a declared-data mutation that no test asserted on because the declaration was not in the test's fixture.","counterconditions":["If the lost mutation was in executable code rather than declared data, the declaration-independence mechanism does not apply.","If the ordinarily-guarded modules' tests assert on behavior derived from declarations rather than declarations themselves, the description claim is weak.","If compile.py's mutation operators include fixture mutation, the invisibility claim fails."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12","quote":"THE THREE SURVIVORS ARE ONE BUG IN TWO COSTUMES: a guard that checks DECLARED DATA does not see ADDED BEHAVIOUR."},{"block":"0615e03ec804152b"}],"mechanism":"The surviving mutations in the ordinarily-guarded modules are all guard-checked declarations. The test describes the declaration because the test imports the declared data and asserts on its shape, not on the behavior that the declaration is supposed to enable. The 1/9 lost mutation under shown-to-fail-first installation is a declaration change that the test fixture did not load, so the test neither constrained nor described it — it was invisible.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.51},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject only when the test's failure mode is not already enumerated in the subject's own error vocabulary; the 1-of-9 loss is a mutation that changed the error vocabulary itself, so compile.py's shown-to-fail-first installation could not find a failing test because the failure was unnameable.","counterconditions":["If the lost mutation was a content-edit hole not affecting any error name, the vocabulary mechanism fails.","If the ordinarily-guarded modules' guards are written in terms of structural patterns rather than named errors, the vocabulary-dependence claim is unsupported.","If compile.py's mutation operators do not include error-name renames, the 1/9 loss cannot be attributed to vocabulary change."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"0148655d7f44f7d5"},{"block":"090c957e3a9cf26a"}],"mechanism":"The subject constrains itself through an error vocabulary (e.g. INV-value-vocabulary, INV-replay-fold). A test that asserts on a specific error name describes the vocabulary. A mutation that alters the vocabulary (e.g. renames an error, changes a selector) is not constrained by any test because the test's assertion references the old name. The distribution 3-of-26 vs 1-of-9 reflects the ratio of vocabulary-stable mutations to vocabulary-altering mutations across modules.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.44},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 1-of-9 loss is not a property of the test at all but of the mutation's blast radius: the mutation was lost because no test in the same repository imports the mutated module under the shown-to-fail-first installation, so the distribution 3-of-26 vs 1-of-9 is an artifact of import graph topology, not of description versus constraint.","counterconditions":["If the lost mutation's module did have a test importer but the test was skipped under shown-to-fail-first, the topology claim is incomplete.","If the import graph saturation is uniform across modules, the observed distribution cannot be explained by topology.","If the 3-of-26 result includes mutations with saturated import graphs that still survived, the topology claim is insufficient."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01ccc04a460b08fc","quote":"It is one repository, one author, one week, and the mutations were proposed by models that had read the acceptance record."},{"block":"0a201dc94a4c3f63"}],"mechanism":"A test constrains a subject if and only if the test's import graph reaches the subject's mutated code path. The 1/9 mutation was in a module with no test importer under the installation order, while the ordinarily-guarded modules had 4/4 to 6/7 because their import graphs were saturated. The asymmetry is a confound: one repository, one author, one week, and the mutations were proposed by models that had read the acceptance record.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.38}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[{"from_ref":"NEW_001","relation_hint":"both point to the same confound: the mutation set is not independent of the acceptance record","to_ref":"NEW_002"}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Speculative: the 1/9 lost mutation might be the same class as the AU-4 erratum — a closed-negative literal that cannot name the generated `discharged(c)`. If compile.py's mutation operator generated such a literal, the test would fail to parse, and the failure would be recorded as a lost mutation rather than a test failure. This would mean the test was never given the chance to constrain the subject.","possible_next_move":"Check whether the 1/9 mutation in the record is a closed-negative literal or a parse-time failure.","why_keep_this":"Links the AU-4 erratum to the mutation-testing asymmetry."},"local_key":"NEW_001"},{"body":{"content":"Counterfactual: if the acceptance record had not been read by the models proposing mutations, the mutation set would have been less targeted. The 3-of-26 result might have been 10-of-26 or 0-of-26. The entire distribution is conditional on the mutation-proposal process, not on the tests themselves.","unfinished":"Would need access to the mutation-proposal prompts to test this.","why_keep_this":"Makes the confound explicit as a counterfactual."},"local_key":"NEW_002"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Does the shown-to-fail-first installation order actually change which test files are executed, or only the order in which failures are reported? If the latter, the 1/9 loss must be explained by something else entirely.","related_refs":[]},{"question":"Can we reconstruct the exact mutation operators used by compile.py from the record? If the 1/9 mutation was not a valid mutation under the operator set, the entire distribution is an instrumentation artifact.","related_refs":[]},{"question":"What would happen if we reversed the installation order for the ordinarily-guarded modules? Would their 4/4 to 6/7 rates drop to the 1/9 level? This counterfactual is not in the record.","related_refs":[]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}